
  lazykube shows pods, workloads, nodes, events and security posture of a Kubernetes cluster in the 
  terminal.                                                                                         
  It is read-only by default; --read-write enables actions for this process, subject to context     
  policy.                                                                                           
         
  USAGE  
         
    lazykube [command] [--flags]                       
            
  EXAMPLES  
            
    lazykube --demo                                    
    lazykube --context staging -n apps                 
    lazykube --read-write --protected-contexts prod    
    lazykube completion zsh > "${fpath[1]}/_lazykube"  
    lazykube man > lazykube.1                          
            
  COMMANDS  
            
    completion [command]  Generate the autocompletion script for the specified shell
    help [command]        Help about any command
         
  FLAGS  
         
    --read-write          Enable actions for this process, subject to context policy
    --protected-contexts  Context names treated as protected, comma-separated
    --read-only-contexts  Context names forced to read-only, comma-separated
    --audit-file          Private local audit JSONL path
    --demo                Run with deterministic synthetic data
    --context             Kubeconfig context to use
    --kubeconfig          Path to the kubeconfig file
    -n --namespace        Limit the view to one namespace (default: all)
    --palette             Accent palette: charm, ocean or matrix (charm)
    --no-anim             Disable animations
    --fps                 Animation frames per second (max 60) (30)
    -h --help             Help for lazykube
    -v --version          Version for lazykube

